<section class="container" style="padding-block:4rem 2rem;max-width:760px;">
  <p class="body-lg reveal" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">The German Construction Industry Federation (Zentralverband Deutsches Baugewerbe, ZDB) represents 35,000 mid-sized, owner-managed construction companies. For its qualification system, the mark was to carry a term that calls the thing by its name: “Meisterhaft” (masterly) — craft qualification at the highest level.</p>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-1" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">In trademark law, precisely this clarity is a problem, not an advantage.</p>
</section>

<section class="container" style="padding-block:1rem 4rem;max-width:900px;">
  <h2 class="display-md reveal" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">The real problem: too descriptive to be protectable</h2>
  <p class="body-lg reveal" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Purely descriptive terms are in principle not registrable as word marks — they must remain free for competitors to use. “Meisterhaft” describes exactly what the seal stands for and on that basis alone would not have been protectable as a word mark. The obvious graphic means — star symbols, familiar from countless rating systems — are equally generic on their own and lack distinctiveness just as much.</p>
  <p class="pull-quote reveal reveal-delay-1">The real achievement did not lie in the design itself — it lay in developing, from descriptive elements that cannot be protected on their own, a mark that is registrable as an overall combination.</p>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-2" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">The result is a combined word and figurative mark whose components remain descriptive on their own, but whose specific combination achieves the required distinctiveness. It uses the latitude of trademark law to the full: as close as possible to the meaningful, descriptive message — for maximum effect — without losing the necessary distinctiveness. Developed for a system available to 35,000 potential users at once, which had to be legally watertight before it was even introduced.</p>
</section>

